{"schema_version":1,"guid":"8904e218-4670-45bf-b38e-e2bfdd82f295","apple_collection_id":"1569037446","show":"Traditional Catholic Audiobooks","titel":"Tertullian - On Shows and On Patience","audio":{"url":"https://anchor.fm/s/5c5ed5d8/podcast/play/52649277/https%3A%2F%2Fd3ctxlq1ktw2nl.cloudfront.net%2Fproduction%2Fexports%2F5c5ed5d8%2F52649277%2F06e97fa7289d0c08381735c5e2281dab.m4a","user_agent":"PodcastsAM/1.0 (+https://amapps.app)","groesse":139251227,"format":"m4a","geprueft_am":"2026-11-22T01:13:52+00:00"},"faehigkeiten":{"clips":false,"clips_aac":true,"clips_suche":false,"range":true,"kapitel":true,"dynamik":"unbestimmt","grund":"Dynamik weder belegt noch ausgeschlossen: keine Merkmale gefunden, aber eine unveraenderte Wiederholungsprobe beweist nichts; M4A: Ausschnitte nur als AAC-Bloecke (clips_aac) - eine App ohne ADTS-Umverpackung zeigt keine"},"einzeiler":"Tertullian outlines why Christians must avoid public shows and practice divine patience.","kurzfassung":"Tertullian argues that Christians must strictly avoid public shows because these spectacles are deeply tainted by idolatry, immorality, and spiritual agitation. He contends that worldly pleasures compromise Christian devotion and baptismal vows, drawing believers away from God. In the second part, he examines patience as a foundational Christian virtue modeled by God and Christ.","stichpunkte":["Public shows are rooted in idolatry through their origins, equipment, and dedicated patrons.","The physical locations of theaters and circuses are defiled by association with demon worship.","Spectacles provoke unchristian passions like rage, lust, and rivalry.","Attending shows violates the solemn renunciation of the devil made during baptism.","Scripture condemns the lust of pleasure just as it condemns other worldly sins.","Patience is presented as a central Christian virtue modeled by God and exemplified by Christ.","Impatience is traced back to the devil and underlies all major human transgressions.","Both spiritual and bodily patience protect believers against the trials and persecutions of the world."],"kapitel":[{"titel":"On Shows: Introduction and Heathen Arguments","start":0.56},{"titel":"The Idolatrous Origin of Shows","start":810.78},{"titel":"The Circus, Theater, and Amphitheater","start":1423.68},{"titel":"Spiritual Agitation and the Condemnation of Spectacles","start":2561.02},{"titel":"The Ultimate Spectacle of Judgment","start":4634.5},{"titel":"On Patience: Divine Example and the Nature of Impatience","start":4888.48},{"titel":"Practical Application of Patience in Life","start":6431.36},{"titel":"The Rewards and Appearance of Christian Patience","start":7557.12}],"clips":[{"hook":"Colors of idolatry in the circus","start":1787.438,"ende":1812.353,"laenge":24.91,"byte_start":30085513,"byte_ende":30483982,"bytes":398470,"range_header":"bytes=30085513-30483982","vorlauf_s":0.0696,"inhalt_start":1787.508,"anker_sha256":"b446b1ee5465e7fa6d354c064ddf1f938a7a98045e93c6ed356db64dcb879c79","anker_ende_sha256":"2bf90b526360b2c70b32b26dd2075121962ede57e14a6c3b1929ca9d0e6a056d","einblendung_ms":60,"text":"Charioteers wore colors that directly represented superstitious and natural elements, starting with white for winter and red for summer, later expanding to represent various deities and seasons.","aac":{"profil":2,"samplerate_index":4,"kanaele":2,"bloecke":[315,330,399,348,376,388,372,378,361,342,384,430,358,336,328,341,390,402,380,391,419,377,318,363,387,362,333,347,343,442,379,349,341,349,349,408,398,356,342,356,353,341,342,351,368,360,389,458,448,428,415,450,302,307,376,424,314,339,339,355,390,357,368,391,360,363,408,447,322,356,338,393,398,369,325,356,346,358,378,379,373,367,370,439,369,360,418,430,333,340,349,393,358,373,345,396,391,380,377,372,378,501,319,342,338,330,362,369,384,433,457,319,322,321,367,390,338,370,376,387,369,352,357,395,406,457,324,352,356,345,348,368,348,330,385,364,355,350,365,455,434,475,319,321,352,367,379,359,364,372,392,354,391,396,37